Direct presentation and partial redraws (Omarchy Mobile)

Qt Quick redraws its whole window for every frame Chromium hands it, and the
Wayland compositor then recomposes the whole window, so a page that changed
a few pixels had the full screen redrawn three times: on a Pixel 7 Pro a
57-pixel animation cost as much GPU and memory power as a full-screen one.

- Chromium redraws only what changed in the buffer it draws into: the output
  device reports partial swaps and, with each swap, what of the next buffer
  is older than the newest frame (viz keeps that area across a skipped swap).
- With a window asking for it (dynamic property `_q_webengine_direct`, an
  alpha channel), the frames go to the compositor as a wl_subsurface below
  the window, each with the area that changed, and Qt leaves a transparent
  hole for them (items above the page still draw over it). Qt draws the
  frames again while the page is faded, scaled, hidden or pictured. A page
  hidden while its window is off screen keeps its last frame there, so a
  picture of the window (an app switcher's card) still shows it.
  libwayland-client is looked up at run time; the protocol objects have an
  event queue and a thread of their own, which also waits for each frame's
  GPU work. QTWEBENGINE_DIRECT_DEBUG=1 logs what it does.

